WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.440
A star can expand until it fills its

00:00:02.440 --> 00:00:05.040
critical lobe. Gas then flows through the inner

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:08.080
point region toward the companion. The donor loses

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:11.100
this material; the receiving star gains it. These

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:14.060
boundaries describe a rotating binary, so the effective

00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:16.820
force balance includes rotation as well as both

00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:17.680
stars’ gravity.

00:00:18.410 --> 00:00:21.650
The transferred gas already carries orbital angular momentum.

00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:23.970
In this disk fed system it curves around

00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:26.890
the compact star, strikes the outer disk and

00:00:26.890 --> 00:00:29.550
gradually moves inward. The impact makes a bright

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:33.090
spot away from the stellar center. Gravitational energy

00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:36.150
released by accretion can power light without restarting

00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:37.550
ordinary core fusion.

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:41.980
The initially more massive star normally evolves first.

00:00:42.140 --> 00:00:44.520
Its lower mass companion can still be on

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:47.080
the main sequence when the primary dies. If

00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:49.540
the binary stays bound, that companion may become

00:00:49.540 --> 00:00:52.060
a giant much later and feed the compact

00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:55.060
remnant. The primary’s death and the later donor

00:00:55.060 --> 00:00:57.040
expansion are separate events.
